WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:08.760
In 1861, a man named Leborgne could say almost nothing beyond one syllable.

2
00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:11.900
Yet he appeared to follow much of what was said to him.

3
00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:13.720
What exactly had broken?

4
00:00:14.783 --> 00:00:19.963
Here is the idea. You can say only what reaches the language network of the speaking

5
00:00:19.963 --> 00:00:22.123
hemisphere, by an unbroken route.

6
00:00:23.117 --> 00:00:25.817
Each bedside test travels part of that route.

7
00:00:26.697 --> 00:00:30.477
In this simple sketch, the pattern of what fails points to the broken stretch.

8
00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:34.820
When you hear a word, both hemispheres analyse its sounds.

9
00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:39.220
Our route starts on the left, at the back of the superior temporal lobe.

10
00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:43.680
Here, roughly in Wernicke's area, the word's sound form is recognized.

11
00:00:44.783 --> 00:00:49.863
One road, the ventral, or lower, road, runs forward and down through the temporal

12
00:00:49.863 --> 00:00:52.883
lobe to meaning. It is how you understand “spoon”.

13
00:00:53.783 --> 00:00:57.183
Run backwards, it finds the sound form of a word you mean to say.

14
00:00:58.167 --> 00:01:03.207
The other, the dorsal, or upper, road, runs up through parietal cortex to the

15
00:01:03.207 --> 00:01:06.747
frontal lobe. It links how a word sounds with how to say it.

16
00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:12.030
There, roughly in Broca's area, a planner assembles the phrase and plans its

17
00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:14.590
movements: lips, tongue and larynx.

18
00:01:15.533 --> 00:01:20.693
Spontaneous speech starts from meaning, finds each word's sound form, and reaches

19
00:01:20.693 --> 00:01:23.853
the same planner by speech-generation connections of its own.

20
00:01:24.713 --> 00:01:26.193
The dorsal road adds support.

21
00:01:27.073 --> 00:01:29.173
The motor system then moves the muscles.

22
00:01:30.267 --> 00:01:33.327
In most people the dorsal road is strongly left-sided.

23
00:01:34.347 --> 00:01:37.427
Understanding single words gets help from both hemispheres.

24
00:01:38.450 --> 00:01:40.750
A clinician runs four bedside tests.

25
00:01:41.570 --> 00:01:43.330
Each leans on a different part of the route.

26
00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:49.260
Fluency is judged from the speech itself: phrase length, rate, effort and grammar.

27
00:01:50.260 --> 00:01:51.620
Several systems contribute.

28
00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:54.460
Comprehension links speech sounds with meaning.

29
00:01:55.750 --> 00:02:00.190
Repetition runs from sound form along the dorsal road to the planner.

30
00:02:01.110 --> 00:02:05.470
Naming runs from a seen object to meaning, to the word's sound form, to the planner.

31
00:02:06.533 --> 00:02:09.213
So damage at many stages can spoil naming.

32
00:02:10.113 --> 00:02:12.813
That is why naming rarely tells the syndromes apart.

33
00:02:13.817 --> 00:02:17.077
In Broca aphasia, speech is slow and effortful.

34
00:02:17.897 --> 00:02:21.417
Comprehension is relatively preserved, but repetition is poor.

35
00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:26.520
The break sits toward the frontal end, where the phrase is assembled and planned.

36
00:02:27.640 --> 00:02:29.240
Speech and repetition pass through it.

37
00:02:29.980 --> 00:02:32.140
Simple comprehension mostly does not.

38
00:02:33.133 --> 00:02:36.653
In Wernicke aphasia, speech flows but conveys little meaning.

39
00:02:37.653 --> 00:02:38.933
Words are swapped or invented.

40
00:02:39.967 --> 00:02:42.467
Comprehension and repetition both fail.

41
00:02:43.347 --> 00:02:47.827
The damage sits toward the start, where both roads begin, and touches several steps

42
00:02:47.827 --> 00:02:48.827
there.

43
00:02:49.067 --> 00:02:54.987
Now you try. Suppose the dorsal road's link from sound to movement is impaired, but

44
00:02:54.987 --> 00:02:56.627
speech generation still works.

45
00:02:57.427 --> 00:02:58.447
Which tests suffer most?

46
00:03:04.450 --> 00:03:06.170
Repetition fails most.

47
00:03:07.070 --> 00:03:11.410
Speech stays fluent, because speech generation still works, but sound errors creep

48
00:03:11.410 --> 00:03:14.470
in. Comprehension is relatively good.

49
00:03:15.510 --> 00:03:16.730
Naming often suffers too.

50
00:03:17.610 --> 00:03:18.950
This is conduction aphasia.

51
00:03:20.733 --> 00:03:25.353
Real words can sometimes be repeated with help from meaning, which still reaches the

52
00:03:25.353 --> 00:03:29.133
planner. Nonwords, like “blick”, lean on the dorsal road.

53
00:03:31.067 --> 00:03:36.147
Suppose instead only the ventral road breaks, from sound form to meaning, and speech

54
00:03:36.147 --> 00:03:37.387
generation still works.

55
00:03:38.287 --> 00:03:39.287
What will she do?

56
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:47.840
Her speech stays fluent, but she understands poorly.

57
00:03:48.780 --> 00:03:51.340
Yet she can echo a sentence she does not understand.

58
00:03:52.240 --> 00:03:56.740
If the cut blocks traffic both ways, naming fails too, and her own speech grows

59
00:03:56.740 --> 00:04:00.300
empty. This is transcortical sensory aphasia.

60
00:04:01.233 --> 00:04:02.793
Dysarthria is different.

61
00:04:03.913 --> 00:04:05.953
Weak or uncoordinated muscles slur speech.

62
00:04:06.853 --> 00:04:09.933
The damage lies in the motor system, beyond the language route.

63
00:04:10.833 --> 00:04:14.293
In isolated dysarthria, comprehension and writing are normal.

64
00:04:15.317 --> 00:04:18.677
The pattern of what fails points to a broken stretch of the route.

65
00:04:19.497 --> 00:04:21.557
It does not name one small patch of cortex.
